      "output": "**Water (H₂O)** is a simple yet remarkably versatile chemical compound that is essential for life and plays a central role in Earth’s physical and biological systems. Here’s a concise overview:\n\n| Aspect | Details |\n|--------|---------|\n| **Chemical Formula** | H₂O – two hydrogen atoms covalently bonded to one oxygen atom. |\n| **Molecular Structure** | Bent shape (≈104.5° bond angle) giving it a polar character. |\n| **States of Matter** | Solid (ice), liquid (water), gas (vapor). Transitions occur at 0 °C (freezing) and 100 °C (boiling) under standard atmospheric pressure. |\n| **Key Physical Properties** | • **High specific heat** – absorbs heat without large temperature changes, moderating climates. <br>• **High surface tension** – allows capillary action and supports life in small organisms. <br>• **Universal solvent** – dissolves many substances, enabling biochemical reactions. <br>• **Density anomaly** – ice is less dense than liquid water, so it floats, insulating aquatic life in winter. |\n| **Biological Importance** | • Constitutes ~70 % of the human body and most other organisms. <br>• Medium for metabolic reactions, nutrient transport, and waste removal. <br>• Regulates temperature through sweating and respiration. |\n| **Environmental Role** | • Drives the hydrologic cycle (evaporation, condensation, precipitation). <br>• Shapes geology via weathering, erosion, and sediment transport. <br>• Supports ecosystems from oceans to rainforests. |\n| **Chemical Behavior** | • Acts as a weak acid (donates H⁺) and a weak base (accepts H⁺), enabling pH buffering. <br>• Forms hydrogen bonds, giving water its unique properties. |\n| **Human Use** | • Drinking, agriculture, industry, sanitation, energy production (steam turbines, nuclear reactors). |\n| **Global Significance** | Covers ~71 % of Earth’s surface; its distribution and quality are critical for climate regulation, food security, and human health. |\n\nIn short, water is a polar, tri‑atomic molecule that exists in all three classical states, possesses extraordinary physical properties that make it a universal solvent, and is indispensable for life, climate, and the planet’s geologic processes."
